WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.058 --> 00:00:02.430
(dramatic music)
(Javelin roaring)

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.260
- [Narrator] This is the Javelin,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.030
an American made missile launcher

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.400
that has been critical
for Ukrainian forces

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.540
in repelling the Russians.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.710
The Javelin's efficacy,
especially against tanks

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.960
has made it a symbol of the resistance.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.060
- There's even a story
about Ukrainian parents

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.890
naming their children, not a joke,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.460
they're newborn child Javelin or Javelina.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.780
- [Narrator] The US has already committed

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.300
8,500 Javelins to Ukraine.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.767
Last year, President Biden
ordered an additional

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:33.840
$653 million worth of Javelins to be sent

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.280
to aid in the war effort.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.230
But Raytheon and Lockheed Martin

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.260
the two primary defense
contractors that make the Javelin

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.090
said that most of what's pledged

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.840
from the US is existing
stocks, not new production,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.610
and that doubling production

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.310
wouldn't be possible until 2026.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.950
- There is no magic formula

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.653
in the supply chain to accelerate this.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.250
- [Narrator] The inability
of US defense contractors

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.190
to quickly replenish
weaponry has raised questions

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.280
about the capability of
America's Defense Industry

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.770
and led some Pentagon officials to argue

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.470
that industry consolidation
has gone too far.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.100
- The challenges with the
Javelin are a wake up call.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.670
- Ukraine has shown that
our approach to acquisition

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.348
since the end of the Cold
War has got a downside.

00:01:21.348 --> 00:01:23.100
(Javelin whooshing)

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.020
- The Javelin and Stinger
missile has become

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.050
the poster child for both
the effects of consolidation

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.903
and indeed the effects
of supply chain problems.

00:01:31.821 --> 00:01:33.630
- [Narrator] In 2021, the US spent

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.960
roughly $800 billion on its defense.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.060
That's more than China, India,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.737
the United Kingdom,
Russia, France, Germany,

00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:46.530
Saudi Arabia, Japan and
South Korea combined.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.960
Given the astonishing
amount America spends

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.430
on its Defense Industry

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.850
why can't the US arm
the Ukrainians faster?

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.410
The answer lies partially

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.903
in how America procures its weapons.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.410
This graph charts the
consolidation that's taken place

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:02.940
within the defense sector.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.510
In the early 1990s, the US
relied on 51 primary contractors.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.660
Today that number is just five.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.270
Lockheed Martin, Boeing, Raytheon,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.880
Northrop Grumman and General Dynamics.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.170
- The fundamental reason

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.720
that defense companies
decided to consolidate

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.420
after the Cold War was that
department told them to do so.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.730
The department was going to be downsizing

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.410
in terms of troops and equipment

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.620
so therefore there was
a need for them to merge

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.590
to address that declining market.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.230
- There was not a major competition

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.360
with a great power the
way the US had faced

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.380
with the Soviet Union.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.710
So in that sense, if we take
ship building for example

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.580
there was a less of a
need to build major ships.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.930
- [Narrator] This trend of consolidation

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.610
took place throughout the industry.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.970
Tactical missile suppliers
declined from 13 to 3.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.010
Fixed wing aircraft suppliers declined

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.270
from eight to three.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.330
Satellite suppliers have
halved from eight to four

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.810
and builders of surface ships
has dropped from eight to two.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.640
- The market in the
defense industrial base

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.220
is very different from the
commercial marketplace.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.530
The difference is, is that the government

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.160
is both the customer and the
regulator, but it's much harder

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.500
than a commercial
marketplace where you have

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.530
a virtually unlimited market.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.200
- [Narrator] According
to a 2022 DoD report,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.480
consolidation has left the US reliant

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.460
on a small number of contractors

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.810
which has stifled innovation

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.700
and undermined price competition.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.530
- A lot of contracts, maybe as many

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.490
as 40% of DoD contracts
are in fact sole source.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.520
That's either because there
isn't another competitor or

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.700
because the existing
manufacturer is just an incumbent

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.700
and is such advantages because
they've been doing it so

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.516
long that there's not really
another viable competitor.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.160
(Javelin whooshing)

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.290
- [Narrator] The Javelin
is a case in point.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.140
- Rocket motors, which are
used in Javelin missiles

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.600
there's only really two producers

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.710
of those motors, those
engines, Aerojet Rocketdyne

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.500
and now of Northrop Grumman,
there used to be five,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.200
six suppliers and now you're down to two.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.456
And obviously if something
goes wrong at one of them

00:04:06.456 --> 00:04:09.180
then that puts a lot
of burden on the other

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.715
and it just slows down the entire chain.

00:04:11.715 --> 00:04:14.280
(machine whirring)

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:15.780
- [Narrator] Ramping up Javelin production

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.040
is also difficult

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.020
because of the just in time supply chain.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.320
- Scaling up production for
the Javelin is a challenge

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.510
and it's a challenge
because the way we procure

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.570
and the way we produce the the
Javelin, there are many kind

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.210
of single and sole source
suppliers for a lot

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.080
of these components.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.420
The companies that produce
these, they produce them

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.225
for a specific contract
need, and producing more

00:04:37.225 --> 00:04:40.170
than that is not optimal
from a business sense.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.450
- The supply chain challenges

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.780
in part because of Covid
has slowed everything down.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.000
So it's actually what
it's done is it's extended

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.715
the time it takes on
average to produce systems

00:04:51.715 --> 00:04:54.810
in some cases to two, three years.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.626
- [Narrator] A 2023 CSIS report found

00:04:57.626 --> 00:05:00.630
that the US is dangerously unprepared

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.240
for a major conflict
such as a war with China

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.590
in the Taiwan Strait.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:08.320
- The reality is that in a
war, according to our war games

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.980
and many that have been done within

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.860
the Department of Defense, both
classified and unclassified,

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.380
the US runs out of key munitions

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.910
like long range precision strike

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.300
in less than a week of a
Taiwan Strait conflict.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.300
The problem is we don't have
enough, not of everything,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.160
but of some very specific weapon
systems that are important

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.350
for the kinds of conflicts
that we're likely to face

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.240
with China and that are important to

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.940
deter those conflicts from even happening.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.800
- [Narrator] Today, many
of the defense sector's

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.430
supply chains were built
to fight the war on terror.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.310
- In terms of acquisitions,
the US focused on

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.310
components that were important
to target the Islamic State

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.430
or the Taliban or
Al-Qaeda around the globe.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.520
Some very specific weapon
systems like drones

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.790
and didn't require the kinds of stockpiles

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.910
of munitions that are important
for a conventional war.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.451
The Ukraine war was a very stark reminder

00:06:06.451 --> 00:06:11.280
that a conventional war is
a war of industrial bases.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.390
Countries involved in a
conventional war, they churned

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:20.074
through munitions, they
churn through weapon systems

00:06:20.074 --> 00:06:22.830
at a much more rapid pace than we had seen

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.823
during the counter-terrorism
campaigns after 9/11.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.060
- [Narrator] In response
to a request for comment,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.560
Raytheon wrote our industry

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.350
has experienced unprecedented
supply chain challenges

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.850
over the past two years.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:37.920
However, by leveraging our scale

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.170
we have been able to
provide significant support

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.230
to our suppliers, and we're
already seeing some components

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.728
like chips and electronics
become more readily available.

00:06:46.728 --> 00:06:50.370
(Javelin whooshing)

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.290
Which brings us back to the Javelin

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:55.110
and a proposed merger of two contractors.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.730
Aerojet Rocketyne which makes
Javelin motors and L3Harris.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:03.540
- L3Harris and Aerojet,
which has agreed to the deal.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.570
Both argue that by
combining and being part

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.320
of a bigger company that
Aerojet can access resources,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.270
access extra investment

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.880
which will help it
perhaps catch up quicker.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.170
- [Narrator] But some politicians,

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.720
like Senator Elizabeth Warren disagree.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.250
In a tweet earlier this year

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.300
she took aim at the proposed
$4.7 billion merger,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.100
arguing that further consolidation

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.833
would undermine competitiveness
within the sector.

00:07:30.037 --> 00:07:32.280
(Javelin whooshes)
(soldiers cheering)

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.760
It's unclear if the Aerojet
Rocketdyne-L3Harris deal

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:36.930
will go through

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:38.850
especially given that a year ago,

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.800
the Federal Trade Commission torpedoed

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.770
Lockheed Martin's attempt to
purchase Aerojet Rocketdyne

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:45.450
on antitrust grounds.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.610
- Lockheed accounted
for just about a third

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:51.270
of Aerojet's sales, that
was turned down ultimately

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.460
by federal antitrust regulators.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.650
They just basically didn't trust

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.080
that Lockheed would
continue to make things

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.080
for other people and not just for itself.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.430
L3Harris is different.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.100
It's a very tiny customer
of Aerojet at the moment

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.320
and it'll be moving
into areas like missiles

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.270
where it's currently just not present.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.570
So it would in theory, emerge
as a stronger competitor

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.176
to Lockheed Martin and Northrop Grumman.

00:08:15.176 --> 00:08:16.710
(missiles whooshing)

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:18.570
- [Narrator] As war continues in Ukraine

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.609
and the threat of other
conflicts around the world loom,

00:08:20.609 --> 00:08:23.760
policy makers must now
grapple with constraints

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.700
of consolidation and a supply chain built

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.590
for yesterday's threat environment.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.590
(Javelin whooshing)

